.TH LLAMA\-CACHE\-MANAGER 1 2026\-08\-21 "llama\-cache\-manager 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
llama\-cache\-manager \- fetch, inspect and delete GGUF models in a llama.cpp or Hugging Face model cache
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B llama\-cache\-manager
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B llama\-cache\-manager ls
.RI [ filter .\|.\|.]
.br
.B llama\-cache\-manager rm
.RI [ options ]
.IR reference .\|.\|.
.br
.B llama\-cache\-manager prune
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B llama\-cache\-manager pull
.RI [ options ]
.IR org / repo : quant .\|.\|.
.br
.B llama\-cache\-manager update
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ org / repo .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B llama\-cache\-manager
reads a model cache in the Hugging Face layout, the one llama.cpp fills when it
downloads with
.BR \-hf ,
and lists, deletes or adds to what is in it.
.PP
Models are named as
.IR org / repo : quant \fR,
the form huggingface.co shows and
.B \-hf
accepts, so a reference can be copied between the three without translation.
.PP
.B ls
is the default command, so the bare program lists everything.
.PP
Every command that deletes or downloads prints its plan first and then asks.
What a removal plan says it reclaims is what the file system gives back: a blob
counts only when every entry pointing at it is going away, so deleting a file
that another revision still uses reports that it reclaims nothing. What a
download plan calls a transfer is what has to come over the network, which is
less than the size of the artifact whenever the cache already holds part of it.
An update shows both halves before it starts, and prints what the removal
reclaimed once the new files are there, because a blob the new revision points
at as well is not freed by dropping the old one.
.SH REFERENCES
A reference has a repository part and an optional quant part.
.TP
.I org/repo
A whole repository, for example
.IR unsloth/Qwen3.8\-27B\-GGUF .
.TP
.IR org/repo : quant
One artifact, for example
.IR unsloth/Qwen3.8\-27B\-GGUF:UD\-Q4_K_XL .
.TP
.IR models\-\-org\-\-repo
The spelling used on disk, accepted as well.
.TP
.BI : quant
A quant in any repository. Usable as a filter for
.BR ls ,
refused by
.B rm
and
.B pull
because both name their repository.
.TP
.I word
A bare word, matched against organisations, repositories and quants.
.B rm
accepts it while it fits one repository, and names the candidates when it fits
several.
.B pull
refuses it, because the hub cannot be searched from here.
.PP
Matching ignores case. The quant is what remains of the file name once the
repository name and the shard counter are taken out, which is the string the hub
shows as the quantisation of that file. All parts of a split file share one
reference, so a model in three parts is one artifact. Where two files of the
same name sit in different directories of a snapshot, each keeps its directory
in the reference, as
.IR :Q4_K_M/model .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " dir" "\fR, \fP" \-\-cache\-dir " dir"
Read
.I dir
instead of the default cache. See
.B ENVIRONMENT
for how the default is found.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " when"
.IR auto ,
.I always
or
.IR never .
The default,
.IR auto ,
colours a terminal and nothing else.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and stop.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help and stop.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS ls [filter .\|.\|.]
List repositories, their revisions and the artifacts in them. Also reachable as
.BR list .
A filter is a reference or part of one, and several filters widen the listing.
Under a filter the sizes describe the rows shown.
.TP
.BI \-\-sort " key"
.IR name ,
.I size
or
.IR age .
.I name
runs first letter first,
.I size
and
.I age
start with the largest and the newest.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reverse
Turn that order around.
.TP
.B \-\-brief
One line per repository, without revisions or artifacts.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the listing as data. See
.B JSON OUTPUT
below.
.TP
.B \-\-warnings
Print what the scan skipped instead of counting it.
.SS rm [options] reference .\|.\|.
Remove a repository, or one quant from it. Also reachable as
.BR remove .
.TP
.BI \-\-until " spec"
Keep to the revisions older than
.IR spec .
This is
.B prune \-\-until
held to the repositories named here.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan and stop.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the plan as data. Removing then needs
.BR \-y ,
because nobody is there to answer a prompt.
.SS prune [options]
Remove what nothing can reach any more: a revision no name points at, a blob no
revision points at, and an interrupted download. None of these can be selected
by
.IR org/repo ,
so removing them takes nothing away.
.TP
.BI \-\-until " spec"
Also remove revisions older than
.IR spec .
Those may still be in use, which is why the plan is shown and the question
asked.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan and stop.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the plan as data. Removing then needs
.BR \-y .
.SS pull [options] org/repo:quant .\|.\|.
Fetch one quant of a repository from the hub into the cache. The quant has to be
named, because the hub cannot be searched from here and the quants of one
repository differ by tens of gigabytes; a reference without one comes back with
the choices named. A file the cache already holds is not fetched again, so
pulling a second quant of a model transfers only what is new.
.PP
A gated or private repository needs a token, which is read from
.B HF_TOKEN
or from the file
.B hf auth login
writes.
.TP
.BI \-\-revision " rev"
Fetch the branch, tag or commit
.IR rev .
The default is
.IR main .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan and stop.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the plan as data. Fetching then needs
.BR \-y ,
because nobody is there to answer a prompt.
.SS update [options] [org/repo .\|.\|.]
Fetch a newer revision of what the cache already holds. With no arguments every
repository the cache follows is checked, otherwise only the ones named. The
check costs one request per name, so running it over the whole cache is cheap.
.PP
What the cache holds decides what is fetched: the same quants, at the newer
commit. Fetching moves the name to the new revision, which leaves the old one
with nothing pointing at it, and that revision is what an update removes.
Nothing else goes: a revision that was already detached, a stray blob or an
interrupted download were not made by this run, and
.B prune
is the command for those. A revision a second name still reaches, such as a tag
fetched at the same commit, stays as well.
.PP
An update takes repositories, not quants. Fetching one quant would move the
name and leave every other quant behind in the revision the update then
removes, so a
.BI : quant
is refused rather than obeyed.
.PP
A repository the update cannot follow is left alone with a reason, and the
others still update: one that no longer offers a quant the cache holds, and one
the hub will not serve at all, because it was taken down, went private, or
wants a licence nobody accepted. A hub that gives no answer stops the run
instead, because calling a cache up to date when nothing was checked would be a
lie.
.PP
A revision a second name still points at, such as a tag fetched at the same
commit, is kept. Repositories the cache holds without a GGUF file are passed
over: the hub cache is a shared directory, and an update that fetches nothing
for such a repository removes nothing of it either.
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Keep the revisions the update replaces. They are detached afterwards, so
.B prune
will offer them.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan and stop.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the plan as data. Updating then needs
.BR \-y ,
because nobody is there to answer a prompt.
.SS completions {bash|zsh|fish}
Print the completion script for a shell on standard output. The script is
generated by the program, so nothing has to be installed next to it.
Completion offers references read from the cache as you type.
.SS help [command]
Print the help of the program, or of one command.
.SH CUTOFFS
.B \-\-until
takes a span counted back from now, or a date.
.PP
Spans are written as a number and a unit:
.IR 30s ,
.IR 30m ,
.IR 12h ,
.IR 7d ,
.IR 2w ,
.IR 6mo ,
.IR 1y .
The unit may be spelled out, and a trailing
.I ago
is allowed, as in
.IR "2 weeks ago" .
.PP
Dates are read as local time:
.I 2026\-07\-01
or
.IR "2026\-07\-01 18:30" .
.PP
Ages come from the modification time of the file, which is when it was
downloaded. Access times are not used, because mount options such as
.I relatime
make them unreliable.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
.B \-\-json
prints byte counts and Unix timestamps rather than formatted text, and carries
a
.I schema
number. The document is written before the program exits, so
.B ls \-\-json
that matched nothing prints an empty list of repositories and still exits 3. The
fields are described in
.I json\-schema.md
in the documentation directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 1
Error.
.TP
.B 2
Wrong usage.
.TP
.B 3
Nothing matched, or no cache directory.
.TP
.B 4
Cancelled at the prompt.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The cache is looked for in this order, and
.B \-\-cache\-dir
overrides all of it.
.B pull
writes into the cache the other commands read, so that a download and the
listing after it cannot land in different places. On a machine with no cache at
all it creates
.I ~/.cache/llama.cpp
rather than the hub cache, because that is the one
.B llama\-server \-hf
reads.
.TP
.B LLAMA_CACHE
Read first, because it is what llama.cpp itself reads.
.TP
.B HF_HOME
.I $HF_HOME/llama.cpp
is used when that directory exists.
.TP
.B HF_HUB_CACHE
The Hugging Face hub cache, used last, after
.I ~/.cache/llama.cpp
has been tried.
.TP
.B HF_TOKEN
The token
.B pull
sends to the hub. Read by
.IR huggingface_hub ,
which also looks in the file
.B hf auth login
writes.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Any value turns the colour off, as does a pipe.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <cache>/models\-\-<org>\-\-<repo>/snapshots/<commit>/
The files of one revision, as symlinks into
.IR blobs/ .
.TP
.I <cache>/models\-\-<org>\-\-<repo>/blobs/
The file contents, shared between revisions.
.TP
.I <cache>/models\-\-<org>\-\-<repo>/refs/
The names, such as
.IR main ,
each holding a commit hash. A revision no name points at is detached.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
List everything, biggest repository first:
.PP
.RS
.EX
llama\-cache\-manager ls \-\-sort size
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find where one quant is cached:
.PP
.RS
.EX
llama\-cache\-manager ls :UD\-Q4_K_XL
.EE
.RE
.PP
See what removing one quant would free, without removing it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
llama\-cache\-manager rm \-n unsloth/Qwen3.8\-27B\-GGUF:UD\-Q4_K_XL
.EE
.RE
.PP
Clean out what nothing can reach:
.PP
.RS
.EX
llama\-cache\-manager prune
.EE
.RE
.PP
Free space by age, after looking at the plan:
.PP
.RS
.EX
llama\-cache\-manager prune \-\-until 30d
.EE
.RE
.PP
See what fetching a quant would transfer, without fetching it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
llama\-cache\-manager pull \-n unsloth/Qwen3.8\-27B\-GGUF:UD\-Q4_K_XL
.EE
.RE
.PP
Fetch a model and its projector in one go:
.PP
.RS
.EX
llama\-cache\-manager pull unsloth/Qwen3.8\-27B\-GGUF:UD\-Q4_K_XL \\
    unsloth/Qwen3.8\-27B\-GGUF:mmproj\-BF16
.EE
.RE
.PP
See what has an update, and what following it would cost:
.PP
.RS
.EX
llama\-cache\-manager update \-n
.EE
.RE
.PP
Follow one organisation, keeping the old revisions for now:
.PP
.RS
.EX
llama\-cache\-manager update \-\-keep unsloth
.EE
.RE
.PP
Turn on completion for the current shell:
.PP
.RS
.EX
source <(llama\-cache\-manager completions bash)
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hf (1),
